A||N||19||fierohuddy||Clutch question I bet no-one can answer ?!?||||8||
Z||000000||fierohuddy||08-14-2001||02:19 AM||||The short of it: Sunday replaced the clutch with the assistance of a mechanic.  Finally got things rolling and now, the pressure in the pedal won't stay.  If I pump the clutch and build pressure, the clutch will shift nicely but once you drive for say 2 min in one gear, there is no resistance in the pedal and you must jam it into neutral then pump the clutch 5 or 6 times to build enough "pressure" to allow it to go into gear.  The every once and a while, I seem to be unable to downshift into second gear... and to top it all off, second gear grinds.<P>Both myself and my mechanic fiend are stumped.<BR>ANY SUGGESTIONS ???<P>The long of it:<BR>To add to it, the slave and the master cylinder were not unhooked during the clutch installation procedure, the engine was not dropped as we ahd use of a hoist and a tranny jack.  And wwhen you start off in furst gear turning to the left, there is often a little rattle from the engine compartment.  Finally, when we went to instll the new clutch, it appeared that the clutch provided by Lordco was the wrong one (The bearing was wrong).  We assumed that it was a 4 cyl clutch, measured and used it anyway with the old bearing repacked.<P>HELP!?!?!?!!?!<P>Thanks,<P>Fierohuddy||||reg||8
Z||000001||KRMFiero||08-14-2001||02:57 AM||||try bleeding it, the bleeding thign is on the slave cylinder<P>::falls asleep::||||reg||1
Z||000002||recluse||08-14-2001||04:19 PM||||You HAVE to bleed it. Ive had more than my share of clutch hydralic problems.  When its grinding it means the clutch is not disengaging fully.  If bleeding it doesnt help then first check your clutch release arm, they have been known to crack or break, i even had a push rod break on me once.  If those are OK then you may need a new master or slave cylinder, with my personal experiences its always been the slave.  you can get rebuild kits as long as the inside of the cylinder is in good shape, i rebuilt a slave and it was still pulling air in so i had to get a whole new one.  When bleeding, the one man vaccum bleeders work pretty good. Good luck<P>matt 87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||08-14-2001||04:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierohuddy:<BR><B>... and to top it all off, second gear grinds...Both myself and my mechanic fiend are stumped.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>...Speaking of topping it off, have you checked the fluid level in the  clutch master cylinder?  If it's consuming fluid, then I think you've developed a leak.  <P>If the new clutch parts cause the piston in the slave cylinder to operate at a different location in the bore, maybe it found a leaky spot somewhere in its new range of travel?    <P>Or, on the other hand, if your mechanic is <B>really</B> a fiend as you say, maybe he sabotaged something? J/K  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||08-14-2001||04:27 PM||||Sounds like something was srewed up during the installation.  Who was the weakest link?  goodby||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodney||08-14-2001||05:00 PM||||You need a new slave cylinder. As a clutch wears the piston in the slave receds farther into the bore exposing raw steel with brake fluid on it. Brake fluid is very corrosive and the walls rust heavily. When you put in a new clutch you push the piston back out and over the heavy rust ruining the rubber piston. It also may bind in the bore. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||grinthock||08-14-2001||06:50 PM||||HEheh I had the same problem.... Here is what I went through with the Fiero Shop<P>found out the Master was gone (The upper and lower parts of the cylinder were leaking into each other) so we replaced that... bleed, NO dice.<P>Replaced the slave to be sure, bleed, No dice.<P><BR>Bleed -- No dice<P>Bleed -- No dice<P>Bleed the system completely 3 times consecutivly (Air came out the whole time) and finally it was FINE....  The air bubbles get cought in the lines and the fluid actually goes AROUND the bubbles, gotta work them all out.... We ran something nuts like 5 LITRES of fluid through it a couples times.....<P><BR>Have fun.||||reg||1
Z||000007||TooManyFieros||08-15-2001||02:09 PM||||First, check the travel on the slave. When someone pushes the pedal, the slave should move 1.1 to 1.2". If you don't have that, bleed it. Read V8Archies page for a good procedure on this. If the slave or master are not leaking, you probably don't need parts, just a good bleed job. This can make you crazy until you bleed it properly. If you are pumping it up, you have air in the system. Please let me know if this helped you.I'm curious what you find.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jncomutt||08-15-2001||02:40 PM||||I got a clutch question off of this.  My slave cylinder corroded inside and jammed up from sitting for a few years.  Me, the moron, tried pushing hard on the clutch pedal, and bent it.  So, I replaced the Master cyl to be safe, the slave cyl, got a new steel pedal.  I then went through 3 clutch arms (they kept splitting at that hole) until I bought a cast Iron one.  Now, my clutch seems to work and engage fine.  The problem is that sometimes the clutch moves soo easily, like sliding on ice.  But sometimes I can barely get it to the floor.  Its just very hard to push, feels like it doesn't want to move.  I found that if I beat on it (drop the clutch at like 4k RPM) or shift hard from 1 - 2, it will be easy again.  Any ideas what this could be???<P>Oh yea, its starting to slip now so its gotta get replaced, I was just wondering if theres anything else I should be checking once its all apart...  TIA||||reg||1
Z||000009||c m west||08-15-2001||04:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierohuddy:<BR><B>The short of it: Sunday replaced the clutch with the assistance of a mechanic.  Finally got things rolling and now, the pressure in the pedal won't stay.  If I pump the clutch and build pressure, the clutch will shift nicely but once you drive for say 2 min in one gear, there is no resistance in the pedal and you must jam it into neutral then pump the clutch 5 or 6 times to build enough "pressure" to allow it to go into gear.  The every once and a while, I seem to be unable to downshift into second gear... and to top it all off, second gear grinds.<P>Both myself and my mechanic fiend are stumped.<BR>ANY SUGGESTIONS ???<P>The long of it:<BR>To add to it, the slave and the master cylinder were not unhooked during the clutch installation procedure, the engine was not dropped as we ahd use of a hoist and a tranny jack.  And wwhen you start off in furst gear turning to the left, there is often a little rattle from the engine compartment.  Finally, when we went to instll the new clutch, it appeared that the clutch provided by Lordco was the wrong one (The bearing was wrong).  We assumed that it was a 4 cyl clutch, measured and used it anyway with the old bearing repacked.<P>HELP!?!?!?!!?!<P>Thanks,<P>Fierohuddy</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>your transmission by chance wouldnt be an Izuzu 5-speed would it?||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierohuddy||08-15-2001||04:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by c m west:<BR><B> your transmission by chance wouldnt be an Izuzu 5-speed would it?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey cm west,<P>Nope... it's a 4 sp.<P>Simon<P>By the way, I considered all the options and have asked my mechanic to bleed the %$&^&( out of it if he can even crack the bleed valve on the slave, then look for leaks in the whole system as many of you described.  After that I will start replacing parts with Genuine GM Parts as recommended,  I'll let you all know.<P>Thanks,<P>Simon<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Patrick86GT||08-15-2001||06:09 PM||||Really, take a look at Archie's lesson on this bleeding thing...you'll get accurate info.   <BR> <A HREF="http://home.v8archie.com/archism.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.v8archie.com/archism.htm</A> <P>BTW, changing a Fiero clutch is a lot of work to go to and re-use the old throw-out bearing.  My experience has been that the bearing is used-up about the same time as is the disc/pressure plate.  Just my 2 cents.<P>Hope you get it resolved.||||reg||1
Z||000012||c m west||08-15-2001||06:27 PM||||is the slave cylinder in alignment with the rod coming out of it? it is possible if the piston inside the slave is forced to opperate at a binding angle that the aluminum piston will wear down and let air into the system in minimal ammounts, therby creating the problem you are describing - just another possiblity||||reg||1
Z||000013||SLYGUY||11-06-2001||01:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by recluse:<BR><B>You HAVE to bleed it. Ive had more than my share of clutch hydralic problems.  When its grinding it means the clutch is not disengaging fully.  If bleeding it doesnt help then first check your clutch release arm, they have been known to crack or break, i even had a push rod break on me once.  If those are OK then you may need a new master or slave cylinder, with my personal experiences its always been the slave.  you can get rebuild kits as long as the inside of the cylinder is in good shape, i rebuilt a slave and it was still pulling air in so i had to get a whole new one.  When bleeding, the one man vaccum bleeders work pretty good. Good luck<P>matt 87 GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I had a similar problem. Master Cylinder was blown. I also used a MityVac one man vacuum bleeder and was much quicker. Will really build up your forearm muscles too!!!<P>Sly<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||revin||11-06-2001||02:18 PM||||ok  my turn.<BR>I am about to change the clutch...Good cheap ideas on a good clutch and throw out berring, and slave? 5 sp.gertag 88||||reg||1
Z||000015||Patrick86GT||11-06-2001||09:59 PM||||REVIN, for the most part, ya get what ya pay for.     cheap IS cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Formula88||11-06-2001||10:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierohuddy:<BR><B> Finally, when we went to instll the new clutch, it appeared that the clutch provided by Lordco was the wrong one (The bearing was wrong).  We assumed that it was a 4 cyl clutch, measured and used it anyway with the old bearing repacked.<P>HELP!?!?!?!!?!<P>Thanks,<P>Fierohuddy</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Care to elaborate there?  Are you talking about the throw out bearing, the input shaft bearing, what?  The input shaft bearing has a sleeve on it (at least on Getrag 5-speeds), but I've had parts stores try to sell me one without the sleeve.  You may want to double check.  If that is the case, the Fiero Store is the only place I've been able to find the correct bearing.||||reg||1
Z||000017||WKDFIRO||11-06-2001||11:33 PM||||Rattle from the tranny and it changes tone when you turn?<P>Get ready to be sad.<P>My 85 GT had a rattle and it did the same thing until West Coast Fieros took out the burned out throw out bearing....the rattle will only be getting worse. ||||reg||9
Z||000018||SLYGUY||11-07-2001||04:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TooManyFieros:<P><B>If you are pumping it up, you have air in the system. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This was the info that I was looking for. <P>When I replaced the master cylinder I bled the system with a Mityvac like FORever. Finally the tension felt great in the pedal and the clutch was disengaging nicely. I was still getting a lot of air out of the system but I decided to quit while I was ahead. Bad decision. I didn't get all of the air out , AND I didn't bleed the slave. (Sounds nasty! LOL) I couldn't find the arm that extends from the slave to the pressure plate. I found it beneath the heat shield/wrap on the exhaust.<P>Two problems:<BR>1) Bleed the system entirely. <BR>2) Bleed the slave cylinder. <P>I didn't do either and now I'm gonna have to!<P>Wish me luck..<P>Sly<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||MrPBody||11-07-2001||09:22 PM||||Here's another <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/015204.html" TARGET=_blank>possibility</A>.<BR>||||reg||1
